<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>{{ t("welcome.page.title", "Welcome — targum") }}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
{{ styles('tokens.css', 'shared.css', 'chrome.css', 'words.css', 'arrival.css') }}
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ script('keep.js') }}
<script>window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";</script>
</head>
{#- The arrival, on a page of its own: three questions, one a screen, as the FirstRun
    boards draw them (design.md §12, "The arrival is three plain questions", 2026-10-09).
    Home sends a reader who has opened nothing here (`home.js`), and `arrival.js` sends
    anybody it has nothing to ask straight back. The site's head and pill are
    included for the scripts that expect them, and not drawn (`arrival.css`). -#}
<body class="welcome">
{% set here = 'welcome' %}
{% set title = '' %}
{% include '_nav.html.j2' %}
{{ script('building.js') }}

<main id="page">
  {#- The bare head the FirstRun boards draw (David, 2026-10-09: the boards win): the
      lockup, and where they are in words on the right. No places, no bell, no account,
      no pill and no foot — there is nothing to go to yet, and the pill covered the
      press at the foot of a phone. The lockup is the one way out, home. -#}
  <header class="arrival-head">
    <a class="arrival-brand" href="/" aria-label="targum">
      <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
        <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <span>targum</span>
    </a>
    <button type="button" class="arrival-back-mark" id="arrival-back-mark" aria-label="{{ t("learn.arrival.back", "Back") }}" title="{{ t("learn.arrival.back", "Back") }}" hidden><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 5l-7 7 7 7"/></svg></button>
    <p class="arrival-step" id="arrival-step"></p>
    {#- EN · RU, for a Russian reader whose browser did not say so: Latin letters, so a
        reader who does not read Russian sees none (design.md §12, 2026-09-28). Drawn by
        `arrival.js` only while the interface question is not asked. -#}
    <p class="arrival-switch" id="arrival-switch" hidden></p>
  </header>
  <section class="arrival" id="arrival" hidden aria-labelledby="arrival-step">
    {#- The language the page speaks, before anything that has to be read (2026-09-20),
        asked only of a browser that may read Russian (2026-09-28). Each row in its own
        name, and the question in every language it offers, because a reader who cannot
        read the next question cannot be asked it. Not one of the three: it is the
        OnboardRuUi boards' "1 of 4". -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-language" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-language"></h1>
      <div class="arrival-opts arrival-tongues" id="arrival-tongues" role="group" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks-language"></div>
    </div>
    {#- 1. Which language they are learning, each in its own greeting and wearing how far
        along it is (design.md §12, "A language wears how far along it is"). -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-learning" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-learning">{{ t("welcome.learning.asks", "Which language are you learning?") }}</h1>
      <div class="arrival-opts arrival-langs" id="arrival-langs" role="radiogroup" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks-learning"></div>
      <p class="arrival-note" id="arrival-later">{{ t("welcome.learning.later", "You can add a second language later.") }}</p>
    </div>
    {#- 2. What they like reading about: a few subjects, any number of them. -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-subjects" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks">{{ t("welcome.likes.asks", "What do you like to read about?") }}</h1>
      <p class="arrival-hint">{{ t("welcome.likes.hint", "Pick as many as you like. We use them to choose your first texts.") }}</p>
      <div class="arrival-opts arrival-doors" id="arrival-doors" role="group" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks"></div>
    </div>
    {#- 3. How much they read, in plain words and in that language's own texts. -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-level" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-level"></h1>
      <p class="arrival-hint">{{ t("welcome.level.hint", "We use this to pick your first text. You can change it later.") }}</p>
      <div class="arrival-opts arrival-levels" id="arrival-levels" role="radiogroup" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks-level"></div>
      <button type="button" class="arrival-unsure" id="arrival-unsure">{{ t("welcome.level.unsure", "I’m not sure, show me a page") }}</button>
    </div>
    {#- 4 and 5, for a Hebrew reader: which of these words do you know (design.md §12, "The
        arrival asks which words you know", 2026-11-01). Two rounds on one screen, the
        second chosen from the first's ticks; a few of the words are made up. Tapping
        marks a word for the estimate and never puts it on the reader's list. -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-check" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-check">{{ t("welcome.check.asks", "Which of these words do you know?") }}</h1>
      <p class="arrival-hint" id="arrival-check-hint">{{ t("welcome.check.hint", "Tap the ones you know. A few aren’t real words, so leave any you’re unsure of. It helps us choose what you read first.") }}</p>
      <ul class="arrival-words" id="arrival-words" lang="he" dir="rtl" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks-check" aria-describedby="arrival-check-hint"></ul>
    </div>
    {#- After the check, and only after it: Skip goes straight home. -#}
    <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-thanks" hidden>
      <h1 class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-thanks" tabindex="-1">{{ t("welcome.check.thanks", "Thanks.") }}</h1>
      <p class="arrival-hint">{{ t("welcome.check.thanks-note", "We’ll choose what you read first from this. You can mark any word as you read.") }}</p>
    </div>
    <p class="arrival-foot">
      <span class="arrival-foot-note" id="arrival-foot-note"></span>
      <button type="button" class="arrival-back" id="arrival-back" hidden>{{ t("welcome.back", "← Back") }}</button>
      {#- Why Continue is asleep on the level step, said to the eye and, through
          `aria-describedby`, to a screen reader (targum-internal#523): it said nothing,
          and a press that does nothing reads as broken. `arrival.js` shows it. -#}
      {#- How many words are tapped this round, on the check's two screens. -#}
      <span class="arrival-tapped" id="arrival-tapped" hidden></span>
      <span class="arrival-why" id="arrival-why" hidden>{{ t("learn.arrival.choose-level", "Choose a level to continue.") }}</span>
      <button type="button" class="arrival-skip" id="arrival-skip" hidden>{{ t("welcome.check.skip", "Skip") }}</button>
      <button type="button" class="arrival-done" id="arrival-done" disabled>{{ t("learn.arrival.continue", "Continue") }}</button>
    </p>
  </section>
  <p class="arrival-waiting" id="arrival-waiting" role="status">{{ t("welcome.page.opening", "Opening…") }}</p>
</main>


<script>
window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";
window.TARGUM_INTO = {{ into|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_LANGUAGES = { {% for code, name in languages %}"{{ code }}": "{{ name }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} };
window.TARGUM_STAGES = {{ stage_table()|safe }};
{#- The words check's lists and round sizes (`check.page_data`). -#}
window.TARGUM_CHECK = {{ check|tojson }};
{% if strings %}window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};
{% endif %}</script>
{{ script('strings.js') }}
{{ script('charts.js') }}
{{ script('lang.js') }}
{#- What was pressed on the way in (targum-internal#127): a `control` event carries a name
    and the window's width, never which text. Silent signed out and where turned off. -#}
{{ script('events.js') }}
{{ script('sync.js') }}
{{ script('account.js') }}
{{ script('shelf.js') }}
{{ script('scenes.js') }}
{{ script('arrival.js') }}
</body>
</html>
